  "problem": "Find the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ about $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=1\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x-1)\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{3}$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1,$ we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(1)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(1)}{3!}(x-1)^{3} \\\\ {P}_{3}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(1)}{6}(x-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(1),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(1),$ ${f}^{\u2033}(1),$ and ${f}^{\u2034}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \u27f9 &  & f(1) & =\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}=1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =-\\frac{2}{{1}^{3}}=-2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =\\frac{6}{{1}^{4}}=6 \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-\\frac{24}{{x}^{5}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2034}(1) & =-\\frac{24}{{1}^{5}}=-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(1),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(1),$ ${f}^{\u2033}(1),$ and ${f}^{\u2034}(1)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =(1)+(-2)\\cdot (x-1)+\\frac{(6)}{2}\\cdot (x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(-24)}{6}\\cdot (x-1)^{3} \\\\  & =1-2(x-1)+3(x-1)^{2}-4(x-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
